<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Incirlik, 2021</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;B&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Incirlik, 2021&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;The Collapse of NATO’s Southern Front&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;CMANO Version 1: 16 November 2019&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;CMO Rebuild: 24 November 2019&lt;/P&gt;&lt;U&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;General Information&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/U&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This is actually a fairly low complexity scenario just with a lot of units. Details and possibilities. The basic premise, &lt;U&gt;although maybe a bit farfetched&lt;/U&gt;, is Turkey continues its slide from NATO and the West with increasingly bitter relations growing with the United States/POTUS and increasingly warm relations with Russia/Putin. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;After the American 2020 election leads to a second Trump Administration and a landslide for Conservatives in the House and Senate, Turkey’s Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an decides the time for leaving NATO has come, but he also decides the 50 B61 nuclear weapons, stored in Incirlik Air Force Base under the "NATO Nuclear Sharing Program" should belong to Turkey. Understanding an American response would be forthcoming to seizing the weapons he increases his ties with Putin’s Russia. Putin seeing the century’s old Russian dream of Russian bases in Turkey, and especially vital naval bases, seizes the opportunity with Russian munitions flowing into key Turkish bases. However, Putin plays this hand cautiously and Russian forces do not actually deploy to Turkey, instead waiting to see how things transpire over the next few months. Still the munitions for a rapid Russian deployment to Turkey are in place, and the Americans are not going to stand for it, nor for the seizure of her personnel and weapons. POTUS and the National Security Council (NSC) order Commander Sixth Fleet to take action to destroy the weapons and any facilities necessary at Incirlik AFB.&lt;/P&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;U&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Missions/Parameters&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/U&gt;
&lt;P&gt;1. &lt;U&gt;Above all&lt;/U&gt; destroy the Twenty (20) B61 nuclear bomb bunkers (underground bunkers = Hardened Bunkers) in the NATO Compound. This is where the points necessary for victory come from (5,000 Victory Points [VPs] per bunker and 100,000 VPs total).&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;2. Degrade/destroy other Turkish facilities and equipment. Some points can be garnered here but not enough to bring you victory (about 10,000 VPs total). This could make up VPs for any U.S. losses though.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;3. Oh yeah, losses! Avoid causalities/Losses at all costs, sorry but the American public has little appetite for large losses to their sons and daughters. Loosing personnel (as represented by the equipment) will cost you points!!!! Loose a carrier and a Triumph is pretty much over (-2,500 VPs for each level of damage light, moderate, heavy and destroyed [-10,000 VPs total])! Some folks don’t like this in my scenarios, if this isn’t to your tastes, there are plenty of other great scenario designers out there for you to play rather than my humble efforts. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;4. Avoid conflict with the Russian Forces (unfriendly at the start). You get ZERO points for destroying Russian forces and in fact loos VPs big time is you sink a Russian sub!&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;5. Oh yeah, don’t sink the other neutral submarines in the area either! I strongly suggest you make ALL ASW Missions Weapons Tight!&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;6. Sinking merchants, the sinking of any merchant, even accidently, will cost the U.S. VPs due to the adverse public relations! &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;7. You have a six (6) hour "pre-scenario" window to decide on weapons loads for your aircraft and get your (randomly teleported) units into better position. To be clear, YOU then decide when to start hostiles but you have six (6) hours to set load outs as you want them and then UP TO 12 ½ hours of night engagement. If you fire early, to get more time, that’s your choice Commander. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;8. ALL submarines teleport (USN, Russian, Turkish and Neutral) to maintain randomness in submarine deployments.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;9. Starting postures toward the United States…&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;a. Turkey, "Unfriendly"&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;b. Russia, "Neutral" (really they’re really "Unfriendly" but they engage too easily on that setting, so I set them to "Neutral."&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;c. Other Nations, "Neutral"&lt;/P&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;U&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Timeline&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/U&gt;
&lt;P&gt;15 July 2016, a coup d'état was attempted in Turkey against state institutions, including the government and President Recep Tayyip Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an. The coup is quickly put down but relations between the United States and Turkey are strained when Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an accused the head of United States Central Command, Chief General Joseph Votel of "siding with coup plotters." Some 300 people were killed, 2,100 injured and at least 40,000 detained. Russia's President Vladimir Putin called Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an out of solidarity before all NATO member countries’ heads following the coup attempt solidifying Russia’s political, economic and military outreach to Turkey.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;July 2016, The U.S. consulate in Turkey issued an advisory to U.S citizens to avoid the Incirlik Air Base in southern Turkey, which houses about 50 B61 nuclear bombs for NATO’s use (Nuclear Sharing, American weapons possibly shared with the host nation during war but using the F version, 12 digit code Permissive Action Link required for arming them remaining under American/USAF control), until "normal operations have been restored. They stated that local authorities were denying access to the air base and that power supplying the air base was shut off. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Twenty-four hours after initial reports that the air base was shut down, United States defense department officials confirmed that the base and its airspace had re-opened to military aircraft and that operations by American aircraft resumed. The Turkish commander of the air base was arrested.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;2 August 2016, in an escalation of tensions with the United States, Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an questioned Turkey's relationship with the United States and criticized the West, claiming that the "script" for the abortive putsch last month was "written abroad". Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an stated that he had personally asked President Obama to extradite the accused Coup leader Muhammed Fethullah Gülen from Pennsylvania to Turkey.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;August 2016, Following the failed coup attempt multiple media outlets published editorials advocating the removal of U.S. nuclear weapons from Incirlik Air base as Turkey was described as being "unstable."&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 2017, Turkish authorities issued an arrest warrant for Henri J. Barkey, former Director of the Middle East Program of the Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars, claiming that he is an agent of the United States Central Intelligence Agency (CIA), that he was present in Istanbul during the 2016 Turkish coup attempt, and alleging that he was one of the key organizers of the attempt. Around 1 December, the Istanbul prosecutor's office issued an arrest warrant for Graham E. Fuller, an American author and political analyst specializing in Islamic extremism after a long CIA career and holding the role of vice-chair of the United States National Intelligence Council. The prosecutor's office based the warrant on suspicion of Fuller helping to plan the coup attempt. Hürriyet stated that Turkish authorities believed that they had traced Fuller's location before the attempted coup and afterwards when he left Turkey.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Late 2017, the president of Turkey and Russian officials signed an agreement worth of $US 2.5 billion for delivery of S-400 air defense system units. The United States immediately decry the purchase claiming it would endanger Turkey’s F-35 purchase. The U.S. puts pressure on turkey to purchase Patriot missiles instead. U.S.-Turkish relations continue to deteriorate. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;January 2018, for weeks, Turkish warplanes bombed the Kurdish enclave of Afrin in northern Syria, killing hundreds of civilians and more than 1,000 Kurdish militia fighters. At least 137,000 people were displaced. President Recep Tayyip Erdogan of Turkey pressed ahead with the offensive despite warnings from the United States that it would further destabilize war-torn Syria. "Beginning from the west, step by step, we will annihilate the terror corridor up to the Iraqi border," Mr. Erdogan said.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;March, 2019, the caliphate falls, the alliance between the Kurds and the United States had its greatest victory in March in Baghuz, Syria, when Kurdish fighters backed by American bombers destroyed the last major ISIS holdout.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;25 June 2019, The United States threatens Turkey with U.S. sanctions over the purchase of the S-400 System. Turkey threatens retaliation. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;12 July 2019, Turkey received its first installment of Russian S-400 missile defense system at its airbase in the capital city of Ankara.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;17 July 2019, Turkey is officially removed from the F-35 program. Relations between the United States and Turkey continue to plummet, while relations between Russia and Turkey warm! The White House issued a statement confirming the move, which Washington had threatened for months. "Turkey’s decision to purchase Russian S-400 air defense systems renders its continued involvement with the F-35 impossible," the White House statement read. "The F-35 cannot coexist with a Russian intelligence collection platform that will be used to learn about its advanced capabilities. Turkey has been a longstanding and trusted partner and NATO Ally for over 65 years, but accepting the S-400 undermines the commitments all NATO Allies made to each other to move away from Russian systems."&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;3 September 2019, Turkish President Tayyip Erdogan said on Wednesday it was unacceptable for nuclear-armed states to forbid Ankara from obtaining its own nuclear weapons. "Many countries have missiles with nuclear warheads, not one or two. But (they tell us) we can’t have them. This, I cannot accept," he told his ruling AK Party members in the eastern city of Sivas. "There is no developed nation in the world that doesn’t have them," Erdogan said.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;6-9 October 2019, Prior to attacking Turkish President Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an called United States President to inform him that Turkey was preparing an imminent invasion of Syria to create a buffer zone between Turkey and Syria, which will enable Turkey to expel the 3 million Syrian refugees living in Turkey. President Trump was warned that United States soldiers in that area would be in jeopardy by the attack. Shortly after on 6 October 2019, the Trump administration ordered American troops to withdraw from northeastern Syria, where the United States had been supporting its Kurdish allies.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;9 October 2019, Turkey launches Operation Peace Spring into northern Syria to create a 20 mile buffer zone between Turkey and the Kurdish PKP. During the day, under pressure from Congressmen and public opinion, Trump sent a letter to Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an proposing to make a deal, otherwise he would destroy the Turkish economy. Erdogan took great offense to the letter, reportedly throwing it in the trash. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;14 October 2019, U.S. and NATO officials discuss the removal of the 50 U.S. nuclear weapons from Incirlik AFB, Turkey. Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an has said he will respond aggressively to western attempts to isolate Turkey and has vowed not to halt the offensive. U.S Officials expressed concern for the continued presence of the weapons, "those weapons," one senior official said, "are now essentially Erdogan’s hostages. To actually fly them out of Incirlik would be to mark the de facto end of the Turkish-American alliance. To keep them there, though, is to perpetuate a nuclear vulnerability that should have been eliminated years ago."&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;9-20 October 2019, U.S. Forces continue to withdraw from Syrian territory, while reports of a Kurdish outreach to Assad and Russia are reported. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Late October 2019, the "Pause" along the Turkish-Syrian Border holds becoming a more permanent cease-fire. American and Turkish tensions settle down, for now. Thoughts of removing the 50 B61 nuclear weapons from Incirlik are shelved. (BTW Wrote &lt;U&gt;BEFORE&lt;/U&gt; it actually happened).&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;February, 2020, Turkey’s second S-400 battery is set up south of Incirlik AFB. Tensions between Turkey and the United States soar! American dependents are quietly urged to head home for "Vacation" and not to return with growing fears of a new "Hostage Crisis!’ The 717th Air Base Squadron at Incirlik and 425th Air Base Squadron at Izmir begin a slow but steady withdraw of personnel. Departing billets are not filled and equipment is slowly but steady loaded onto C-17 transports. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;March, 2020, the Spanish Army’s 74&lt;SUP&gt;th Anti-aircraft Artillery Regiment withdraws its Patriot Missiles from Incirlik. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;April, 2020, 39th Air Base Wing personnel begin a regularly scheduled service of the B61 nuclear weapons under their control, upgrading them to B61-12 standards with new PITs and Pals. Personnel note the close Turkish surveillance of their movements outside of the hardened bunkers. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;July 2020, American President Donald Trump blasts NATO members in a particularly bitter tirade at a campaign rally in Mobile, Alabama. He bitterly blasts France, Germany, Poland, and other NATO allies for not providing enough support and for the first time he expresses his desire for the United States to leave NATO, "instead forming an Alliance with the United Kingdom and other English speaking and likeminded nations." He particularly blasts Turkey and announces new sanctions on that nation for its deployment of the S-400. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;August 2020, Russian President Putin offers an arms deal with Turkey offering to sell them Mig-31 and Su-27 models which would be more compatible with Russian systems allowing for mutual defense between the two nations. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;September 2020, Sanctions bit into the Turkish economy with heavy protests outside the American Embassy and Incirlik AFB. The USAF quietly begins a drawdown of personnel at Incirlik. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;October 29th 2020, Turkish MPs block an American C-17 transport which is observed to moving a B61 weapon off the base. In a 22 hour standoff large trucks are parked on the runway until the weapon is returned to its bunker. With the American election days away this act is largely overlooked but it is clear the Turks are holding the B61s hostage on the base and will not allow their removal. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;3 November 2020, President Donald Trump sweeps in the U.S. Election defeating challenger Elizabeth Warren handily. The House also changes hands with Congress returned to Republican hands. The overwhelming mood in the new Congress will be particularly isolationist. Indeed in his victory speech President Trump announces, "now the time has come to review the errors of the past 75 years! While NATO may have made some kind of sense during the Cold War it has long outlived its usefulness and the United States is horribly taken advantage of. Let Europe sort out its own problems, we don’t need being the backer of a new Nazi regime in Europe!"&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;4 November 2020, outrage over the President’s comments is loud and strident. German Chancellor Angela Merkel stated, "I have long had doubts about the continued American presence on German soil. America can no longer be relied on as an ally and perhaps it is time for a new idea, A Central European idea. The countries of Central Europe might be better served by an alliance of our states rather than continuing an alliance with the United States and without the United States there is no longer a NATO!"&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;24 December 2020, Russian and Turkish aircraft participate in bilateral exercise over the Black Sea with Russian jets landing at Turkish bases in northern Turkey. American President Trump threatens dire consequences and that. "He would totally destroy Turkey if they formed an alliance with Russia."&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;26 December 2020, Carrier Strike Group 8 (CSG 8), centered on the USS Harry S. Truman is ordered from the Gulf of Oman to the eastern Mediterranean Sea. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;27 December 2020, The American Ambassador to Turkey is ordered to leave the country. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;1 January 2021, President Trump orders the USAF to minimize the American footprint in Turkey. That night the first F-22 Raptors and F-15 Eagles from a detachment deployed there depart the base for Italy. American diplomatic personnel begin to fly out of the country. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;2 January 2021, Turkish paratroopers take up position outside the USAF underground nuclear storage bunkers. American personnel are safely, but firmly, relieved of their posts outside of the bunkers and the bunker are safely sealed. The Turks cannot immediately gain access to the weapons, and with the Permissive Action Links, could not use them even if they could. Conversely, the USAF still cannot remove them. A standoff outside the bunkers develops with senior USAF personnel demanding access to the weapons and the Turks setting up machine gun and SAM positions outside of the bunkers.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;3 January 2021, Turkish President Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an makes a nationwide speech were he states, "America has not been a good ally of the Turkish people hurling insult after insult upon us. We are no longer welcome in NATO nor does continued membership in NATO continue to make sense for our great nation. The 50 Nuclear Weapons in Incirlik have been promised to us in the past and now they will be ours. They have been on our soil for decades and we will have them. I have ordered our forces at Incirlik AFB to arrest the American officers and hold them until we are given the codes for those weapons. Turkey is a great nation and we will have our due. Russian Forces have promised to assist us in asserting our independence from NATO and we will take President Putin at his word. Therefore beginning today Turkey will no longer cooperate with NATO and will expel all remaining NATO personnel from our holy land!"&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;4 January 2021, President Trump demands Turkey stand down and release both the nuclear weapons and "our great American heroes" immediately or he will take action to utterly and completely destroy Turkey. "We can easily do it and everybody knows it. It is time for Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an to stop being a tough guy and do the right thing for his nation." It is also announced the United States would be undertaking the closest surveillance on the nuclear bunkers and any attempt to move the weapons out of the bunkers would be met with an immediate nuclear strike on the base. Citizens panic in Adana evacuating the area.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;4-10 January 2021, amid rising tensions and back and forth accusations between Presidents Trump and Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an, United States naval vessels begin to get underway from east coast bases and Marine units begin to load transports at Morehead City, NC.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;5 January 2021, NATO announces neutrality in the Turkish-American dispute and no NATO member will become involved militarily and Article Five will not be applied. President Trump, issues several angry Tweets…&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;"NATO has never been there for us. It’s just like 9/11 where were they then? They just take, take, and take from America."&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;"And Germany, don’t get me started on Germany! Germany is the worst! I have no use for Nazis! That’s what they were Nazis and they’re falling back into Nazism, everyone know it!"&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;American media dispute the accuracy of these Tweets but the political damage is done.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;6 January 2021, Greece announces all American aircraft must depart the Souda Bay Facility immediately and none will be allowed to land until the current dispute is settled. Trump angrily Tweets about "Greek cowardice." &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;7 January 2021, VP-5’s, P-8a Detachment, in Souda Bay, evacuates to NAS Sigonella to join the rest of the squadron. The Italian Prime Minister restricts U.S. forces in Italy to "support roles" only but looks at the Maritime Patrol Aircraft, normally based there, as falling into that category. Still American bombers and multirole aircraft are unwelcome. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;10 January 2021, Turkish Army engineers, with Russian help, gain access to the weapons in one of the bunkers. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;11-15 January 2021, Additional American Forces begin to move toward Turkey as Russian Transports are seen to be offload large numbers of munitions at Turkish AFBs including Incirlik.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;15 January 2021, in a joint announcement Germany, Poland and Denmark all announce they will abrogate their defense treaties and leases with the United States. All American forces and equipment is to be off their county’s respective soil by July 15th 2021. Discussions into forming a new "central European" defense alliance begin.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;16 January 2021, USN CSG 8 transits the Suez Canal. In the coming days a steady stream of American naval vessels and aircraft head toward the region. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;17 January 2021, Turkey grants Russia full overflight rights.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;19 January 2021, on the eve of the American Inauguration Day, imprisoned American officers are put on display by the Turkish regime with a warning that any military action will put their lives at risk. Russian President Putin warns the United States that Russia will defend Russian personnel in Turkey and especially at Incirlik AFB and that no harm should come to them. President Putin will guarantee the safe keeping of the American nuclear weapons and safety of imprisoned American military personnel. True to his word non-essential/nuclear trained personnel are flown to Moscow to be turned over to the American Ambassador for transit to the United States. Remaining personnel, which number 126 officers and enlisted personnel, are held in a barracks building. Russian munitions continue to build up but no further movement of Russian equipment is noted. CIA and DIA estimates are that with the load outs seen to be in revetments, Badger, Backfire, May and Bear aircraft can quickly operate out of Incirlik with some fighter support.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;20 January 2021, President Trump gives Erdo&lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;ğ&lt;/FONT&gt;an until January 31st to release the American personnel and return the 50 nuclear weapons to American control or face "complete and utter destruction." A second American Carrier Strike Group, CSG 10 centered on USS Eisenhower, enters the Mediterranean. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;21-25 January 2021, The "Turkish Nuclear Crisis" continues to escalate with Turkish and USN aircraft engaging in mock dogfights over the eastern Med. Russian forces seem to be moving out of Turkey but strengthening their positions in the Caucasus Peninsula. Turkish naval vessels seem to be in port possibly fearing to come out and possibly face destruction.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;26 January 2021, Russian surface naval forces depart the eastern Mediterranean Sea and the Russian S-400 Transporter-Erector Launchers and stowed in travel position. This is seen as a sign that Russia has no desire to become further embroiled in the current crisis. However, President Putin makes it clear that Russian submarines will remain in the area and he expects their safety to be respected by the Americans. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;27 January 2021, Poland, Germany, Denmark, the Netherlands, Belgium. Luxembourg and the Czech Republic all announce they are leaving NATO as of 1 May 2021 and will form the Central European Treaty Alliance (CETA) in a "NATO like" defense pact limited to Central European nations only. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;28 January 2021, Intense Russian, Turkish and American consultations are reported in Geneva. CNN shows footage of a grim faced American delegation leaving the hotel conference room and getting into limousines. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;The NATO Secretary General, yields to the inevitable and announces the dissolution of NATO as of 1 May 2021. The Balkans, Baltic States and Eastern European nations all scramble to determine how to meet their future defense needs. In Washington, President Trump welcomes the announcement calling NATO, "obsolete and America is better without it. Look at the problems NATO has caused in Turkey. We can develop a better alliance with our friends in England and Canada, and everybody knows it!"&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;29 January 2021, In an unannounced/surprise move, all remaining American personnel in Turkey are placed on several El Al aircraft and flown to Ramstein AFB, Germany where they are released to American USAF authorities. The majority of the merchant shipping and civilian air traffic begin to divert around the eastern Med, but a substantial amount of local shipping remains.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;30 January 2021, Reports on Russian Television (RT) rebroadcast relay that Turkish engineers and scientists gained access to a nuclear bunker last week and may be making progress in breaking the PAL system on one B61-12 they have been experimenting with. President Trump once again warns of a nuclear response and repeats the January 31st deadline.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;31 January 2021, The American deadline is reached. Turkish and American military units all jockey for position across the region. As nightfall approaches in the eastern Mediterranean (afternoon in Washington D.C.), the world holds its collective breath… &lt;/P&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;U&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Designers Notes&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/U&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This scenario has gone through so many changes, I can’t really count them anymore. At one time there were substantial Russian forces but they always engaged and I had to remove them, because it became a general U.S-Russian War rather than an single strike. Then there were three (3) US CSGs but it was just too easy for the USN to win with three (3) so parred them down to two (2). Also it was originally a three (3) day scenario that went down to two (2) days, and then 12 1/2 "operational" hours. So now you have the overnight hours to achieve your mission which, if you think about it, is very consistent with recent U.S. strikes in the region. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;There are a lot of negatives for U.S. losses and sorry Americans just do not react well to significant losses. Consider Operational Eldorado Canyon in 1986 and the outrage over the loss of 1 F-111 crew. I could go on and on but my view is "war is politics by other means," and politicians hate giving people bad news. So a few losses can be overcome but large losses of significant personnel/units will not be tolerated by the American public. As to the high losses for sinking neutrals/Russians well that’s international politics/public relations. Russia and the others are not going to react well to their units being sunk that is reflected with a loss of victory points. The additional losses for a destroyed ferry, well it’s a ferry think that’s pretty obvious!&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;An INI and Delta File are both included for deep rebuilds.&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/SUP&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
